.TH HSH 1 "May 2026" "Version 1.0" "Simple Shell Manual"

.SH NAME
hsh \- a simple UNIX command line interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.br
.B echo "command" | ./hsh

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command line interpreter written in C.
It reads commands from standard input, resolves them using the
.B PATH
environment variable, and executes them using the
.BR execve (2)
system call.

The shell operates in two modes:

.TP
.B Interactive Mode
The shell displays the prompt
.B ($)
and waits for the user to type a command. After each command is executed, the prompt is displayed again.

.TP
.B Non-Interactive Mode
Commands are read from a pipe or redirected file. No prompt is displayed. The shell reads, executes, and exits.

.SH OPTIONS
.B hsh
takes no options. It reads from standard input only.

.SH USAGE

.TP
.B Interactive mode:
.nf
$ ./hsh
($) /bin/ls
($) ls -l /tmp
($) exit
.fi

.TP
.B Non-interactive mode:
.nf
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
$ cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.fi

.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS

.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell immediately. The exit status will be the exit status of the last command that was executed before calling exit. No arguments are handled.

.TP
.B env
Print all the current environment variables to standard output, one variable per line. Each line is in the format NAME=VALUE.

.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
When the user enters a command, the shell follows these steps:

.IP 1. 4
Read the input line using
.BR getline (3).

.IP 2. 4
Parse the line into tokens (command name and arguments) using
.BR strtok (3),
splitting on spaces and tabs.

.IP 3. 4
Check if the first token matches a built-in command
.RB ( exit " or " env ).
If it does, execute the built-in and go back to step 1.

.IP 4. 4
If the command contains a
.B /
character, treat it as an absolute or relative path and check if it is executable using
.BR stat (2).

.IP 5. 4
Otherwise, search for the command in each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable using the internal
.B _which
function.

.IP 6. 4
If the command is
.B not found,
print an error message to stderr and do
.B not
call
.BR fork (2).
The exit status is set to 127.

.IP 7. 4
If the command
.B is found,
call
.BR fork (2)
to create a child process, then call
.BR execve (2)
in the child to execute the command, passing the full argument list and the current environment.

.IP 8. 4
The parent process calls
.BR wait (2)
to wait for the child to finish, then collects its exit status.

.IP 9. 4
The shell displays the prompt again and repeats.

.SH PATH RESOLUTION
The shell uses the
.B PATH
environment variable to find commands. The
.B _which
function splits the PATH by
.B :
and checks each directory by appending
.B /commandname
to it. If the resulting full path is an executable file (checked with
.BR stat (2)),
that full path is returned and used for execution.

If
.B PATH
is not set or the command is not found in any directory, the shell prints an error and skips execution.

.SH ERROR MESSAGES
When a command is not found, the shell prints the following message to stderr:

.nf
./hsh: <line_number>: <command>: not found
.fi

.TP
.B Example:
.nf
$ ./hsh
($) invalidcmd
./hsh: 1: invalidcmd: not found
($) ls
./hsh: 2: ls: not found
.fi

.PP
If
.BR fork (2)
fails, the shell prints the error using
.BR perror (3)
and returns exit status 1.

If
.BR execve (2)
fails inside the child process, the shell prints the error using
.BR perror (3)
and exits with
.BR EXIT_FAILURE .

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
The command was executed successfully and returned exit status 0.

.TP
.B 1
A system call such as fork or execve failed.

.TP
.B 127
The command was not found in PATH or as an executable file.

.TP
.B Any other value
The exit status of the last command that was executed.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B hsh
inherits the full environment from its parent process.
The environment is passed directly to
.BR execve (2)
using the global
.B environ
variable.

The following environment variables affect the behavior of the shell:

.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories in which the shell searches for commands. Example:
.nf
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
.fi

.TP
.B HOME
The home directory of the current user.

.TP
.B USER
The name of the current user.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B main.c
Contains the main shell loop, input reading with getline, argument parsing with strtok, built-in command handling (exit and env), and the execute_cmd function that forks and runs commands with execve.

.TP
.B path.c
Contains the
.B _which
function that resolves a command name to its full executable path by searching through each directory in the PATH environment variable.

.TP
.B shell.h
The header file that includes all standard library headers used by the project and declares all function prototypes shared between source files.

.TP
.B AUTHORS
Lists all individuals who have contributed to this project.

.SH EXAMPLES

.TP
.B Execute an absolute path:
.nf
($) /bin/ls
hsh main.c path.c shell.h
.fi

.TP
.B Execute a command found in PATH:
.nf
($) ls -l /tmp
total 0
.fi

.TP
.B Print environment variables:
.nf
($) env
USER=lama
HOME=/home/lama
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
.fi

.TP
.B Handle a command not found:
.nf
($) notacommand
./hsh: 1: notacommand: not found
.fi

.TP
.B Non-interactive usage:
.nf
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
hsh main.c path.c shell.h
.fi

.TP
.B Exit the shell:
.nf
($) exit
$
.fi

.SH COMPILATION
To compile the shell:
.nf
gcc -Wall -Werror -Wextra -pedantic -std=gnu89 *.c -o hsh
.fi

